WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:05.220
The Cloverfield movie-verse has now officially
expanded into some wild new territory.

00:00:05.220 --> 00:00:09.750
Netflix surprised fans of the sci-fi film
series by dropping the third installment,

00:00:09.750 --> 00:00:13.080
The Cloverfield Paradox, on Super Bowl Sunday
without warning.

00:00:13.170 --> 00:00:17.960
Like the first two films, Cloverfield 3 offers
a new perspective on why all of those giant

00:00:17.960 --> 00:00:19.420
monsters have appeared on Earth.

00:00:19.540 --> 00:00:23.000
If you haven't seen it yet, you might want
to click away now because we're about to take

00:00:23.000 --> 00:00:24.920
a deep dive into outer space.

00:00:25.200 --> 00:00:25.840
Still with us?

00:00:26.220 --> 00:00:26.720
Good.

00:00:26.740 --> 00:00:30.620
The film centers on an international space
station crew who are tasked with trying to

00:00:30.620 --> 00:00:35.430
solve the world's ongoing energy crisis by
testing a particle accelerator known as the

00:00:35.430 --> 00:00:35.930
Shepard.

00:00:36.140 --> 00:00:41.460
The mission is critical, as widespread starvation
and world wars threaten to break out on Earth.

00:00:41.680 --> 00:00:45.620
The crew's miracle machine launch doesn't
quite go as planned, though, as it sends the

00:00:45.620 --> 00:00:47.230
whole ship to another dimension.

00:00:47.230 --> 00:00:51.680
So, how does the film tie everything together,
and what Easter eggs can be found floating

00:00:51.680 --> 00:00:53.000
throughout the space module?

00:00:53.120 --> 00:00:53.860
Let's take a look.

00:00:54.540 --> 00:00:56.120
The obligatory Kelvin nod

00:00:56.780 --> 00:01:01.040
As the biggest-named producer of all three
films, J.J. Abrams was free to sprinkle in

00:01:01.040 --> 00:01:04.260
tons of in-references to his own life and
previous works.

00:01:04.350 --> 00:01:09.100
Fans of Abrams may recognize the name of the
gas station that Ava Hamilton and her husband

00:01:09.119 --> 00:01:11.999
Michael are waiting on line for at the beginning
of the movie.

00:01:11.999 --> 00:01:16.130
It's called Kelvin, which is a brand that's
appeared in a ton of Abrams' prior projects.

00:01:16.130 --> 00:01:20.229
It was the name of the man who occupied the
hatch in Lost; it was the name of a spaceship

00:01:20.229 --> 00:01:25.020
in Star Trek; and even Star Wars: The Force
Awakens included a place called Kelvin Ridge.

00:01:25.040 --> 00:01:29.940
The name is a nod to Abrams' grandfather Henry
Kelvin, who was very influential to his interest

00:01:29.940 --> 00:01:30.540
in movies.

00:01:30.560 --> 00:01:35.420
The Kelvin gas station logo previously appeared
in a scene from 10 Cloverfield Lane, so it

00:01:35.439 --> 00:01:38.220
also makes sense to include it here for continuity's
sake.

00:01:38.220 --> 00:01:42.240
And that's not the only bit of brand loyalty
Abrams snuck into the pic, either.

00:01:42.660 --> 00:01:44.040
The Slusho connection

00:01:44.380 --> 00:01:49.679
Another common thread found in most J.J. Abrams
projects is Slusho, a fictional brand of iced

00:01:49.679 --> 00:01:53.820
drinks that seems to pop up everywhere in
Abrams' universe, from his hit small screen

00:01:53.820 --> 00:01:56.140
series Alias and Fringe to Star Trek.

00:01:56.260 --> 00:02:01.539
It also appears in the first Cloverfield film:
Jason Hawkins sports a shirt with the logo

00:02:01.540 --> 00:02:04.500
as an homage to his brother Rob's new gig
with the company.

00:02:04.560 --> 00:02:09.000
But their hand in the havoc that levels the
planet might be heavier than fans anticipated.

00:02:09.080 --> 00:02:13.459
According to the in-universe lore, the brand
is owned by a Japanese mining company called

00:02:13.459 --> 00:02:18.019
Tagruato which is heavily featured in the
Cloverfield Alternate Reality Game, and is

00:02:18.020 --> 00:02:20.820
said to have set up offshore oil rigs throughout
the world.

00:02:20.909 --> 00:02:25.160
The company name is also listed on a letter
sent to Howard Stambler in 10 Cloverfield

00:02:25.170 --> 00:02:29.250
Lane, which led some Easter egg hunters to
believe their drilling platforms might've

00:02:29.250 --> 00:02:32.769
opened up some kind of conduit for the original
monsters to emerge through.

00:02:32.769 --> 00:02:37.400
Digital materials later revealed during the
run-up for 10 Cloverfield Lane offered details

00:02:37.400 --> 00:02:41.730
of Stambler's job working with satellites
and the company's satellite launch — which

00:02:41.730 --> 00:02:46.340
detail-oriented fans suspected had gone south
and opened up the floodgates of monsters.

00:02:46.360 --> 00:02:51.000
However, the events of The Cloverfield Paradox
seem to challenge the long-held notion that

00:02:51.000 --> 00:02:53.120
a satellite failure started this whole thing.

00:02:53.300 --> 00:02:58.260
Alternatively, the presence of a Slusho bobble
head doll in the spaceship, along with a sighting

00:02:58.260 --> 00:03:03.200
of the actual company logo on-board, suggests
that Tagruato has also been funding the Shepard

00:03:03.200 --> 00:03:03.700
project.

00:03:03.860 --> 00:03:08.260
So that means, yes, they're still totally
responsible for all of this, just not in the

00:03:08.260 --> 00:03:09.160
way people thought.

00:03:09.320 --> 00:03:14.800
In the last shot of Cloverfield - the original
movie - there appears to be a falling... something.

00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:18.520
Perhaps that's the escape pod seen at the
end of The Cloverfield Paradox?

00:03:18.920 --> 00:03:20.200
A brotherly bond?

00:03:20.560 --> 00:03:25.400
Another common thread The Cloverfield Paradox
weaves with the previous sequel is the last

00:03:25.400 --> 00:03:29.640
name of another prescient conspiracy theorist
showcased in the movie, Mark Stambler.

00:03:29.760 --> 00:03:35.020
Yes, Stambler is the last name of John Goodman's
character in 10 Cloverfield Lane, and he,

00:03:35.020 --> 00:03:39.040
too, seems to have some advanced knowledge
of the hellfire that would rain down on Earth.

00:03:39.120 --> 00:03:43.480
Mark Stambler is seen giving a news interview
about his book, which shares a title with

00:03:43.499 --> 00:03:48.640
this movie, and promoting his Twitter handle
"Paradox is Real" -- which is also real, albeit

00:03:48.640 --> 00:03:49.140
inactive.

00:03:49.360 --> 00:03:52.960
"This experiment could unleash chaos."

00:03:53.260 --> 00:03:58.439
Some suspect the two characters might be related,
or even brothers, as it's certainly no coincidence

00:03:58.440 --> 00:04:01.580
that they are equally impassioned about their
beliefs in this end-of-days event.

00:04:01.720 --> 00:04:06.160
Howard Stambler's bunker guest-slash-prisoner
Emmett even makes fun of him in 10 Cloverfield

00:04:06.160 --> 00:04:09.540
Lane by poking fun at his theory about "mutant
space worms."

00:04:09.660 --> 00:04:12.940
That joke becomes all too real in Paradox
when our Russian comrade Volkov gets eaten

00:04:12.940 --> 00:04:19.100
from the inside out after the ship's earthworms
are teleported into his body during the accidental

00:04:19.100 --> 00:04:19.980
dimensional hop.

00:04:20.560 --> 00:04:21.740
A familiar face

00:04:22.300 --> 00:04:26.240
If the connection between the Stamblers wasn't
clear enough, there's an even closer connection

00:04:26.240 --> 00:04:28.920
drawn between them by way of actress Suzanne
Cryer.

00:04:29.020 --> 00:04:33.340
In The Cloverfield Paradox, she portrays the
broadcast journalist who's seen interviewing

00:04:33.340 --> 00:04:36.280
Mark Stambler, and if she looks familiar,
there's a reason.

00:04:36.400 --> 00:04:40.700
She also appears briefly in 10 Cloverfield
Lane as the injured woman who's desperately

00:04:40.700 --> 00:04:43.640
trying to gain entry into Howard Stambler's
bunker.

00:04:43.640 --> 00:04:49.080
"To clarify, you believe the effort to solve
the world's energy crisis might unleash demons?"

00:04:49.300 --> 00:04:50.440
"She's begging me."

00:04:50.440 --> 00:04:51.020
"Let me in."

00:04:51.020 --> 00:04:53.260
"Do not let her in!"

00:04:53.740 --> 00:04:56.420
Too bad she didn't listen to her special guest
that day, huh?

00:04:57.300 --> 00:04:58.380
A mama monster?

00:04:58.880 --> 00:05:03.380
In unearthed production notes for the first
film, J.J. Abrams, director Matt Reeves and

00:05:03.390 --> 00:05:07.730
screenwriter Drew Goddard talked about the
theories and themes that went into the film,

00:05:07.730 --> 00:05:10.540
and Abrams made an interesting comment about
the monster:

00:05:10.540 --> 00:05:11.540
"He's a baby.

00:05:11.540 --> 00:05:12.240
He's brand new.

00:05:12.240 --> 00:05:14.660
He's confused, disoriented and irritable.

00:05:14.760 --> 00:05:18.340
And he's been down there in the water for
thousands and thousands of years."

00:05:18.440 --> 00:05:22.470
Given that Clover could level a skyscraper
without even flexing, it might've been hard

00:05:22.470 --> 00:05:27.030
for fans to conceive of this creature as anything
even remotely resembling a baby, but the final

00:05:27.030 --> 00:05:30.380
shot of The Cloverfield Paradox makes that
very possible now.

00:05:30.640 --> 00:05:34.260
Whereas Cloverfield's Manhattan-shredding
monster was as big as any building on the

00:05:34.260 --> 00:05:38.450
island, the monster that's shown ripping through
the clouds in the final scene looks to be

00:05:38.450 --> 00:05:40.100
as big as some small countries.

00:05:40.100 --> 00:05:43.740
Which means they've either opened another
dimension of craziness with that second Shepard

00:05:43.740 --> 00:05:46.880
overload...or Clover's mother has come out
to play now, too.

00:05:47.760 --> 00:05:49.080
Foreshadowing part four?

00:05:49.660 --> 00:05:53.680
Mark Stambler's words during his on-air interview
should've been more harrowing to everyone

00:05:53.680 --> 00:05:56.350
who watched, especially those on the space
station.

00:05:56.350 --> 00:06:00.430
However, they tuned him out and got right
to the job of doing exactly what he was warning

00:06:00.430 --> 00:06:04.610
against because, in their minds, the mission
of solving the world's energy crisis was far

00:06:04.610 --> 00:06:08.560
more pressing than reading his promoted book
to see if he was making any sense.

00:06:08.640 --> 00:06:13.880
But one thing he did say that, in retrospect,
seems important is how wide-reaching the interdimensional

00:06:13.880 --> 00:06:15.460
warps of the Shepard would be.

00:06:15.460 --> 00:06:16.800
He told the reporter:

00:06:16.960 --> 00:06:21.020
"...they risk ripping open the membrane of
space time.

00:06:21.020 --> 00:06:24.800
Smashing together multiple dimensions, shattering
reality."

00:06:25.020 --> 00:06:29.420
"In the past, in the future, in other dimensions."

00:06:29.660 --> 00:06:33.960
That certainly gives the Cloverfield movie
universe unlimited room to grow, and movie

00:06:33.960 --> 00:06:39.010
four, Overlord, is expected to center on a
World War II-era encounter involving American

00:06:39.010 --> 00:06:41.340
paratroopers and a Nazi-occupied village.

00:06:41.460 --> 00:06:45.340
Stambler's suggestion that the past could
be affected by their space flub seems like

00:06:45.340 --> 00:06:49.950
a clever way of keeping the film in the Cloverfield
universe, too, despite hopping back in time

00:06:49.950 --> 00:06:50.940
a few decades.

00:06:51.480 --> 00:06:52.520
Thanks for watching!

00:06:52.620 --> 00:06:55.420
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:06:55.420 --> 00:06:57.710
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

